[Credits]
<div class="box box-1">
  This disassembly would have taken a lot longer to finish if it weren't for the
  research and documentation already done and made available by:
#LIST
  { <a href="https://mrcook.uk/">Michael R. Cook</a> }
  { tcdev, for the annotated disassembly of Knight Lore that Michael converted
    to SkoolKit }
LIST#
  This game shares a lot of functionality with Knight Lore, hence that
  disassembly was a great help.
</div>

[Expand]
; Draws the graphic of a strip: a byte for the number of rows followed by two
; bytes of pixels for each row, with the first row at the bottom.
#DEF(#STRIP(addr,attr=$07)(fname)
  #LET(stripheight=#PEEK($addr))
  #LET(strippages=({stripheight}+$07)/$08)
  #UDGARRAY(2,attr=$attr,scale=4,step=2,flip=2)((($addr)+$01)-(($addr)+{strippages}*$10)-$01-$10){$00,$04*(($08-({stripheight}%$08))%$08),$40,$04*{stripheight}}($fname)
)

; Draws the graphic of a strip whose rows follow the address of the macro.
#DEF(#STRIPDATA()(fname)
  #STRIP(#PC-$01,$07)($fname)
)

; Draws the graphic of a strip of wall from the table of wall strips.
#DEF(#WALLSTRIP(id,attr=$07)(fname)
  #LET(wallstrip=#PEEK($6E36+$id*$02)+#PEEK($6E37+$id*$02)*$100)
  #STRIP({wallstrip},$attr)($fname)
)

; Draws a sprite of the given size, which is stored as a mask byte and then a
; graphic byte for each row of each character, with the first row at the bottom.
; A set bit in the mask means that the sprite covers the background, so the
; masks are copied inverted into an unused page of memory for SkoolKit.
#DEF(#MASKED(addr,cols,rows,attr)(fname)
  #LET(pages=($rows+$07)/$08)
  #PUSHS
  #FOR($00,$cols*$rows-$01)||n|#POKES($F800+n,$FF-#PEEK($addr+n*$02))||
  #UDGARRAY($cols,attr=$attr,scale=4,step=$cols*$02,flip=2,mask=2)((($addr)+$01)-(($addr)+{pages}*$10*$cols)-$02-($cols*$10):($F800)-($F800+{pages}*$08*$cols-$01)-$01-($cols*$08),$cols){$00,$04*(($08-($rows%$08))%$08),$04*$cols*$08,$04*$rows}($fname)
  #POPS
)

; Draws a sprite from the table of sprites.
#DEF(#SPRITE(id,attr=$07)(fname)
  #LET(spriteaddr=#PEEK($6E9E+$id*$02)+#PEEK($6E9F+$id*$02)*$100)
  #LET(spritecols=#PEEK({spriteaddr})&$0F)
  #LET(spriterows=#PEEK({spriteaddr}+$01))
  #MASKED({spriteaddr}+$02,{spritecols},{spriterows},$attr)($fname)
)

; Draws a sprite whose rows follow the address of the macro.
#DEF(#SPRITEDATA()(fname)
  #LET(spritecols=#PEEK(#PC-$02)&$0F)
  #LET(spriterows=#PEEK(#PC-$01))
  #MASKED(#PC,{spritecols},{spriterows},$07)($fname)
)

[VillageMap]
<p>
The map of the village is stored at #R$5E04. It is #N$20 cells wide and
#N$20 cells high. Each cell holds a tile from #N$00 to #N$23. Tile #N$00
is open ground, which shows as the black streets in the picture, and
tile #N$01 is the solid tile that fills the edge of the village and
much of the rest of it. The other tiles are the trees, graves, walls
and other things that stand in the village.
</p>
<p>
The picture is drawn from those tiles in the same way as #R$D372, with
the view not turned round. The colour of each wall is taken from the
lowest two bits of the tile (see #R$D356): bright green, cyan, yellow
or white. The picture is four times the size of the Spectrum pixels:
drag to move, and use the mouse wheel or pinch to zoom.
</p>
#HTML(<div class="village-map-viewer" id="village-map-viewer"></div>)
<p class="village-map-hint">Drag to move. Scroll or pinch to zoom.</p>
